    "case_summary": "A 23-year-old male was referred by his general dental practitioner. One year prior, an x-ray revealed a cyst associated with an ectopic lower right canine tooth. The patient had an uneventful medical history and no notable intraoral clinical findings, except for the absence of tooth 43. Radiographic examination showed a 3 cm unicystic radiolucent area with clear boundaries. Tooth 43 was situated at the base of this area. There was no evidence of root apex resorption. The lesion was surgically removed under general anesthesia and the cavity was filled with pelvic spongiosa. The separation of the lesion from the mandibular bone was uncomplicated. The postoperative recovery was uneventful.\n\nThe tumor is solid and there is a cyst formation. The epithelium is in the form of whorled masses of spindle cells as well as sheets and plexiform strands. Rings of columnar cells give rise to duct-like appearance. Calcification is sometimes seen and may be extensive.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
